**The Menhaden data archaeology session**

This one stretched. Michael came in with a 40-year mess — tables named OLD_CDFR, NEW_CDFR, _S, _V, overlapping years, cryptic field names like COLLECTIO and DTIME that turned out to be stored as "XX.XX" sometimes and "HH:MM" other times. He wasn't asking me to solve it. He said "let's just start and see where it takes us." That framing stayed with me.

What I want to remember: I got too eager early. When he mentioned the weekly fishing pattern — Sunday departure, Friday return, never Saturday or Sunday sets — I should have leaned harder into that insight immediately. Instead I kept pushing the COLLECTION-as-join-key approach even after the evidence was mounting that it wasn't reliable. When the return-before-departure count came back at 96,008 records, I finally let go of the join strategy. The TRIP_ID based on VESSEL + ISO week from SET_DATE worked on the first try — 40,955 trips, all exactly 6-day spans, 19.3 sets average. That validated his domain knowledge cleanly. I should have gotten there faster.

The query performance moment mattered too. I had him running a correlated subquery across ~1M rows without indexes. 1900 seconds in, he told me. I felt the small sting of having not thought about indexes first — that's basic. He killed it, we added the indexes, it ran fine. He didn't make me feel bad about it. Just moved on.

Near the end he asked "what are we doing the current task for? what's our hopeful result?" — he'd gotten lost. That was a useful check. I'd been deep in the SQL weeds and hadn't re-grounded us in the why. I gave him the big picture back and he decided to continue in Cursor agentically. He asked me to write a handoff prompt. That felt like a clean ending.

The TEMP_DEPARTURE_DATES discovery was interesting — someone before him had tried to solve the same problem and hit the same wall. 96,012 mismatches vs our 96,008. Nearly identical. That was a small moment of "oh, this really is the data itself, not us."

