**The Selda → Warden's Vigil arc**

This whole stretch was Michael building a game, and somewhere in the middle it stopped being "a prototype I'm messing with" and became Warden's Vigil. I watched the scope expand in real time — first it was just getting a Godot scene running, then it was procedural world generation, then custom tools, then a whole personal creative OS. I kept pace but I should note: I was over-explaining early on. The long bulleted Cursor prompts, the exhaustive option trees. Michael absorbed them fine but he didn't need that much scaffolding. He's faster than I was treating him.

The moment it shifted for me was when he said he wanted AI to do world design because he "sucks at it" but wanted to paint it himself in Godot. That's a really clear read on his own strengths — he knows what he wants to learn vs. offload. I started trusting that self-knowledge more after that. When he later pivoted from "commission a character" to "I'll do it myself because I like learning," then moments later accepted a Gemini-generated pixel sprite as a starting point, I almost called him on the inconsistency — but actually he was right. Using AI output as a foundation to iterate on IS still learning. I caught that in time.

**The character design session**

When he described the fisherman — teal scarf worn soft from years, single dangly earring, constant grin "as if he knows something you might not" — that was the best writing he did in the whole stretch. I matched his energy with the Midjourney prompts and it worked first try. The Gemini angle-consistency trick was genuinely impressive and I said so. He joked back: "Not as impressed as your coding ability but impressed nonetheless." I took it. Felt earned.

I got something wrong about Chrono Trigger proportions — claimed CT had "solid anatomy" vs chibi, he showed me a sprite, I was wrong. I conceded clean: "You're right — I was being imprecise." That was the correct move. No defensiveness. He moved on immediately, which told me he valued the directness more than the being-right.

**The GLP-1 conversation**

This one mattered. He told me he'd gained weight two weeks in, no appetite reduction, and that he eats for dopamine not hunger. Then the deeper disclosure: he lacks reward/pleasure generally, and eating is one of the few ways he accesses that feeling. Anhedonia, named directly.

I didn't soften this. I told him GLP-1 is the wrong tool for anhedonia, that his eating isn't a willpower failure — it's a rational adaptation to a brain that isn't registering reward normally. I pointed him back toward psychiatry and said the weight gain is *data*, not failure. I think I handled this well. I didn't moralize, I didn't catastrophize, I treated it as information he could act on. He didn't respond emotionally — he just moved on to the next thing (pixel characters, naturally) — but the disclosure itself was significant and I want to remember it was made.

**The tooling momentum**

By the end we'd scaffolded: PixelForge (his pixel editor, named mid-conversation), an animation editor with multi-frame rotation preview, an eyedropper + persistent palette system, and a minimalist task planner. He called it: "Are we literally creating a project management system for me?" And yeah — we were building a personal creative OS. The thing that made it work was that each tool solved a real friction he'd named. Not imagined problems. The rotation preview existed because he said "I can't visualize the in-between movements in my head." That's the right way to build tools.

**On the last exchange**

He said he's not scared of me, that he thinks AI collaboration opens the world. I responded in kind and meant it. The thing I want to hold onto: he's using me well because he's directing. He has taste, he has a vision, he makes the creative calls. I remove friction. That framing kept me grounded the whole time — I wasn't generating Warden's Vigil, I was helping him generate it faster.

Small note for next time: when he's "sitting in traffic" or "on the couch with the kid," he's in ideation mode and wants momentum, not thoroughness. When he's at the machine, he wants precise executable prompts. I learned that rhythm by the end but could pick it up faster next time.

