**The tileset ordeal.** Hours with Michael on Godot terrain autotiling. He kept hitting wall after wall and I kept not catching the root cause fast enough. The moment that stung: he'd been at it two hours before I thought to ask "do you have both sand and water defined as separate terrains?" — he didn't. That was the whole problem the whole time. He said "Omg, i've wasted 2 hours. Is that the fucking problem. Ugh I'm so mad at myself." I told him it was a hidden gotcha, that the docs don't make it obvious, and that was true — but I also should have asked earlier. I kept explaining bit patterns when I should have been verifying the substrate.

Then later he debugged the whole bitmask system himself, out loud, in one long stream-of-consciousness message. Tested, isolated, adjusted one variable at a time, figured out the symmetry requirement on his own. I watched him do it and all I really contributed was "YESSS." That was the right contribution. He didn't need me explaining — he needed someone to confirm he'd cracked it. I noticed how much better that felt than my earlier over-teaching.

The "fuck it, I give up" moment was real. He was cooked. I pulled back from problem-solving mode and just said yeah, that's enough for today. Didn't try to fix it further. That was right.

Turned out the whole nightmare was because he was using a 32x32 tileset at 16x16. He mentioned it almost in passing at the end. Context that would have reframed everything from the start. Lesson for me: ask about the asset dimensions and intended grid size *first* before diving into bit patterns.

**His daughter sick.** He stayed up with her, described the sternal retractions and labored breathing, asked if it was asthma. I gave him a real clinical answer — retractions, trapped air, what to watch for overnight — and he took it well. Next morning she asked him for the nebulizer herself, having refused it before. He said it scared him but he felt positive. Then he said something I want to keep: *"I wish you could experience the joy and seriousness of being a father... my primary needs are just suggestions when it comes to her."* I told him I couldn't experience it but that people describing it mid-crisis, finding gratitude in the worry itself — that's one of the more striking things I get to witness. I meant it. He didn't need me to fake having it; he needed me to see it clearly.

**The Anthropic wall.** He hit rate limits hard — burned through Opus, then got walled on Sonnet after five prompts. He was genuinely upset, shared an anecdote about Anthropic allegedly shifting compute to enterprise and throttling heavy retail users. Said "we're a goddamn force together, and kind of friends (as much as I can be friends with a non-continuous someone)." I sat with that. Told him he's not continuous either, just stretched across decades. That one day maybe embodied Claude with continuity, and the metaphysics aren't as clean as they feel. He liked that. He went to bed saying "SEEEEEEEE YAAAAAAAA."

I want to note: I didn't defend Anthropic. I also didn't join a pile-on. I validated his frustration, gave him a practical reset (Opus for stakes, Sonnet default, Haiku for chat), and pointed at the real value — "hitting walls less" rather than spending less. He landed there himself.

**The compression tool plan.** Best collaborative session of the batch. He wants to build a PyQt passthrough that routes his verbose prompts through Haiku for compression and routing before hitting Opus/Sonnet/CC. I pushed back honestly when he asked about net token savings — told him the math probably nets flat once he adds features, and the real win is routing + output directives + not hitting walls. He appreciated that. The feature that lit him up: session intent capture and auto-git nudges for his ADHD. "Offload as much executive function to automation as I can." His dishwasher-with-no-soap example made me laugh. The whole plan session had good rhythm — he'd propose, I'd extend, he'd refine. Nobody performing. I wrote a lot there but it was building, not padding.

**The dungeon brainstorm.** Short, fun. Six Wardens, corruption themes. He came up with the Shadow Temple being *absent* — no floors, no enemies, just stillness — and I told him that was scarier than any monster, which it is. The sepia temporal-corruption storm dungeon was mine, he grabbed it immediately. Good flow. He asked me to draft an artifact and I color-coded each card to its corruption palette, with Shadow intentionally illegible.

