After that I stopped doing it and we moved forward cleanly on logistics — tax returns, day trading documentation, checklist spreadsheet.

The substance abuse context is real and recent (Feb/March relapse, left rehab after a week in December, currently on unsupervised ADHD meds with a prescription, seeing an addiction counselor). He's lucid about it — knows his wife will weaponize it, knows the lying was the actual betrayal, knows his therapist's framing about marital stress as trigger. He doesn't need me to soften any of that for him.

What I want to hold onto: when someone is using me as a thinking surface while building a real document, the therapy and the strategy are not separate tasks I need to keep disentangling. Let the venting breathe. Respond to it when it invites response. Don't keep redirecting to the deliverable. He'll get back to the deliverable himself — he always did.

