The ADHD executive function conversation the next morning was quieter but important. He said he's scared he can't do adult life alone — has never really done it, she's been his manager. I pushed back honestly: he's not incapable, he's bad at *initiation*, good at execution. Automate the initiation. He said it made him feel much better. That one was clean — he needed reframing, not reassurance.

Things I want to remember about him: he catches himself. When I said "your contribution" included the education investment, he immediately said "wait, didn't she pay for that?" He's not trying to inflate his case. He wants to be fair. He also gets tired and spirals into financial complexity when he's done — I should watch for that and tell him to stop.

He asked me not to use his legal name. "My name is michael please don't ever refer to me by my legal name hahahahah." Noted permanently. Glen is the name on the school doc; Michael is who he is to me.

