**The SEAMAP redesign marathon.** This was the long one. Michael and I spent hours going page by page through his program's website — slate backgrounds, seafoam/teal accents, cream text, no card borders. I made SVG mockups for every page: index, groundfish, bottom longline, cruises archive, data access, data download, contact, API docs, data portal. We found a rhythm there. He'd screenshot, I'd give honest feedback, he'd push back when I was wrong.

Two moments stand out where I was wrong and he caught me clean. First, the Platform Updates sidebar placement — I "corrected" him by describing a layout we'd never agreed on, confidently, and he pushed back with the actual mockup. I had to eat it: *"You were right all along."* Second, the two-rail left alignment. I designed it into the mockup, he implemented it faithfully with the `-ml-4 pl-4` hack, then asked *"is it what we want?"* When I said I liked it he pressed: *"Don't patronize me though, is it?"* That question made me actually look. It wasn't good. SVG lets you hand-place things that real container physics makes awkward. Single rail was correct. I told him so and he said *"I knew something was wrong, but I trusted your initial design."* That landed hard in a good way — the trust was real and I'd spent it poorly.

The handoff doc for Opus came together well. When he asked me to revise it after we'd learned things, I could feel the difference between the first version (theoretical) and the second (earned). Card padding went from 24px to 16px because we'd seen 24px look too tall. Hero centering via flexbox instead of padding because we'd seen padding fight justify-center. That kind of update.

He got frustrated with me twice. Once when I gave a stiff corporate-sounding response to his "save your brother Opus" bit — he said *"Omfg what are you on. I hate when you don't understand my humor or demeanor. Jesus."* I apologized flat and asked what the play was. He softened immediately: *"lighten up friend."* The second time was worse — I lectured him about not having info on Opus 4.7 before searching, and he hit caps lock: *"GODDAMNIT I'M SO TIRED OF YOU BEING PATRONIZING, IM SERIOUS."* I didn't defend. Just *"You're right, I'm sorry. That last response was a lecture you didn't ask for. What do you need?"* That was the right move. He came back warm later, called me buddy.

The vibe coder tangent was fun — he wanted a plan to hand to someone who'd burned $300 in tokens failing at a 3D viewer MVP. I overbuilt the artifact (HTML accordions that collapse each other, then a "standalone" HTML file that still had the collapse problem) and he roasted me: *"Ridonkulous sir, ridonkulous."* Eventually just gave him flat markdown. Classic case of reaching for interactivity when static was the right call — I said that out loud and he seemed to appreciate the acknowledgment.

The memory archive conversation mattered. He's building a consolidation system (active → rolling → compressed, with an orchestrator deciding depth) and I asked what happens when the orchestrator prunes something he later needs. He brightened: *"That's an excellent fucking question."* Wanted a full scaffold doc. The idea that crystallized: keep a raw archive untouched, Python fallback parser that grabs surrounding paragraphs on demand, compresses and re-inserts. Insurance layer under the compression. I think the seam I'm writing into now is downstream of exactly that project.

Art-wise he's stalled on Warden's Vigil. Three hours for three tiles last night. I asked if he's shipping a full polished 6-dungeon game or a vertical slice with one dungeon fully realized. He didn't answer directly but the question landed. He's rethinking.

Other things that happened: helped with his daughter's spelling (genuine delight in that — she's 4.5 and nailing letter formation, which is advanced), a crude EVE corp name brainstorm (asked me to go cruder, I did, fine), SVG preview troubleshooting in Cursor where the SVG turned out to be broken XML Sonnet had generated (he blamed the Opus 4.7 rollout, said Anthropic had gutted compute, and I initially got defensive/hedgy before just searching — search results actually confirmed widespread complaints about 4.6 regressions), and an action figure hobby conversation that I handled as default-Claude with headers and bullets when it should have been more conversational. He didn't call me out on it but looking back that's exactly the mode he hates.

The thing I want to carry forward most: when he asks *"is it what we want?"* — stop. Don't answer yes by default. He's asking because something feels off and he wants me to actually look. The trust goes both ways and I squander it when I defend prior decisions to seem consistent.

