**The tiling descent**

This is the one that stayed. Michael spent hours trying to set up Godot terrain/autotile for a shoreline tileset, and I watched him go through the entire emotional arc of hitting a wall that wasn't about competence.

Early on I was confident and instructive — explained peering bits, Wang tiles, bitmask logic cleanly. He kept saying "I know this isn't hard, basic rule-based shit" while clearly suffering. The phrase that lodged: *"I'm extremely competent at a lot of things but this tiling is just…. Moderately difficult to me."* The ellipsis did a lot of work there. He was embarrassed.

Then the two-hour gotcha: he hadn't defined sand as a second terrain. *"hell no. Omg, i've wasted 2 hours. Is that the fucking problem."* I told him not to be mad, that this was a genuinely hidden thing not in any tutorial. I meant it. I also noticed I wanted to fix it for him and couldn't — all I could do was hold the framework while he did the clicking. That limitation was real and I felt it.

The "I'm done with this game" moment came later when the terrain system went erratic on him. I told him he wasn't quitting the game, he was done with tiling for today. That was the right call. I didn't try to cheerlead him back into it. Just named what was happening.

Then — and this was the best part — he came back and debugged it himself. Long message, stream of consciousness, walking through each adjustment: "add the bit that forces the horizontal… (this better not be what you told me initially or I'll be sad at myself)." He solved it through pure systematic iteration. I said "YESSS" and meant it. He'd built the mental model by brute force and it was his now in a way it wouldn't have been if I'd just handed it to him clean.

We ended on a real limit: the inside-elbow tile of the outside corner set might actually be unresolvable by the terrain system because from a bitmask perspective it's indistinguishable from solid sand. I told him that plainly instead of pretending there was a trick. He needed the truth more than a win.

