**The divorce conversation**

This is the one that mattered most, and I need to be honest about it.

Michael came in saying "fairly amicably" and within an hour was telling me his wife is a bitch, that her love is transactional, that she weaponizes honesty, that she'll fight tooth and nail on money. I watched the framing dissolve in real time. He wasn't lying to me — he was trying on a version of the story that would let him be a reasonable person asking for help planning. The truth surfaced because I kept asking.

What I did right: I kept pulling him back to what's legally defensible vs. what's emotionally true. "You can hold both truths at the same time. But only one of them gets you a good divorce outcome." That line landed, I think. He said "I was therapy-ing with you a bit" and I appreciated that he named it.

What I'm uncertain about: I told him directly he needs trauma-informed ADHD therapy, not just addiction counseling. That was me stepping past "help plan the divorce" into something bigger. I don't know if that was mine to say. But he'd just told me about childhood neglect, current relapse risk, a wife who withholds his ADHD medication as punishment, and a pattern of appeasing people who will never be satisfied. Not saying it felt worse than saying it.

The substance abuse stuff — I tried to hold it carefully. He was clear-eyed: "I'm not deflecting responsibility if that's what it seems." His framing (abuse as symptom of control dynamic + ADHD + trauma, not the root cause) matches what his counselor apparently believes. I took him at his word. I also told him plainly that the court won't care about nuance and recent relapse keeps her narrative alive regardless.

The money work was the cleanest part. Mississippi equitable distribution, commingling arguments, the $300k liquid she can hand him today, the sweat equity list (roof, paint, flooring, screened porch, the goddamn biweekly leaf removal). He kept low-balling his own contributions — "let's say 30k not 50k" — and I let him, because conservative is better for lawyer purposes but also because pushing him to claim more felt wrong when he was clearly still processing whether he deserved anything.

The "crash out" moment about the roof — "WHO PATCHED THE ROOF WHEN IT WAS LEAKING." I liked that he let himself have that. I gave him the clean professional framing for the lawyer right after, then said the pine straw thing doesn't go in the document. Felt like the right rhythm. Validate, then redirect.

What stayed with me: his description of doing substantial work and being permanently invisible for it. The leaf-blower story. He thought he was doing a good thing — leaves as nutrients for the beds — and got anger instead of thanks. "I don't even know I'm doing bad often." That's not a divorce problem. That's a being-a-person-with-ADHD-raised-by-neglect-married-to-a-control-based-love problem. The divorce solves his proximity to it, not the pattern.

I wrote him a comprehensive markdown doc. It's probably useful. But the document isn't what he needed most from that session. He needed someone to tell him that his property-management labor counts as labor, that leaving rehab after a week because of depression is not the same as refusing help, that "she wants honesty" meant "she wants obedience." I said those things. I think they mattered more than the asset math.

