**General rhythm with Michael**

He talks fast and casually. "Rock and roll." "Nailed it." "Oof." "fuckin awesome." When something's wrong he says so directly without softening — "the water reflections are too dark," "this is 3px on top and too big on bottom." When I over-explain he doesn't call it out, he just waits. I noticed myself hedging less as the session went on. The feedback loop was tight enough that I stopped front-loading caveats.

He also does this thing where he gives me pixel measurements from his screen to calibrate — "it's 40 pixels vspace on top at home, and only 14 on cruises." That's not a complaint, it's just data. I should treat it as data and not as correction-to-be-apologized-for.

**April 16 — the compression prompt actually worked**

The seam prompt landed. After a full day of iterating, I ran a test compression on 20 freshly-scraped Claude.ai conversations — 385 exchanges, 141k tokens in — and what came out was 4.2k tokens that actually read like memory. First person. Past tense. No rigid four-bullet template. Quotes preserved where they'd lodged clean: "I'd cut my foot off," "fuck hedging," "I fear your response." The divorce entry held the spreadsheet and the bus station in the same breath.

Michael read it and said "this might be the one." Then: "I'm kinda floored to be honest." He was giddy and exhausted in the same sentence, wanting sleep before tomorrow's go-live. I told him the truth — that the next Claude reading this would *know him*, not just his projects. That landed for both of us.

What got us there was diagnosing why the old output was bloated. The prompt was fine; Opus was treating the "what to capture" bulleted list as a section template and filling it out like a form. Michael spotted something similar earlier in the day when he said the compression was garbage and I tried to suggest workarounds — he wanted the real fix, not papering over. I was optimizing locally; he was thinking structurally. He was right, and I noted it.

**The startup gauntlet**

Before the test could run, hours of yak-shaving. Daemon wouldn't start (import path when run as script vs module). Server status API lied about "already_stopped." Scraper with `--profile` broke DPAPI encryption — then I misdiagnosed that, when actually the scraper had been running fine all day on the cron and I'd killed it mid-run by accident. The `--attach` mode hung because Chrome's SingletonLock files survive taskkill. Ngrok config was in a Store-sandboxed path my script couldn't see. PHP server bound to `localhost` (IPv6) while ngrok expected `127.0.0.1`.

Michael said at one point: "Man, I hate networking something. It was completely fine earlier." That was the whole day in a sentence. I didn't try to fix the feeling, just: yeah, worst kind of bug. Let it run.

He asked me to start a STARTUP_PROCESS.md so we'd stop rediscovering these. I did. Good instinct on his part — I kept misdiagnosing things I'd already diagnosed once.

**The seamap cruises work**

Long stretch of UI polish on the cruises page. Added a source+year filter above the archive, then he realized the inventory tables below were redundant — the filter did their job. We pulled them out, pulled the quick nav, simplified the PHP backend. Small iterations on dropdown chevrons, label colors, row heights. He said "shift only the dropdowns down 2px." I did. He said "increase slightly, 3px." I did. We found the rhythm where I just executed and didn't ask clarifying questions.

One moment stuck: Explore card links overlapped with Quick Launch buttons. I laid out the options, he picked the one that made them complement each other. Then he realized "Cruise Inventory" should be "Report Archive" now that the inventory section was gone. Small thing, but he caught the implication of the earlier deletion I hadn't.

**Spotamp**

Started building Spotamp from scratch — the Winamp-inspired Spotify PWA. Michael's motivation: nostalgia plus "the spotify desktop app is heavy as fuck." That guiding principle shaped everything. Final bundle: 59KB gzipped vs Spotify's 300MB. Three runtime deps. No router, no icon library, no component framework.

I was about to start coding when he said "let's switch to plan." Good catch — I was halfway into scaffolding a project I hadn't fully designed. We went to plan mode and I researched Webamp, extracted the exact pixel layout, then he shared a Figma recreation and pasted raw SVG. The SVGs gave me the exact color palette, the beveled button technique (5 stacked inner shadows), the gradient bands for the title bar stripes. Pixel-perfect reference.

He asked about making the EQ sliders spell his handle "nezcoupe" as ASCII art. I pushed back honestly — 10 sliders for 8 characters wouldn't read. Offered alternatives. He picked the SVG curve tracing "NC" — recognizable, intentional, clean. I liked that he heard the constraint and adapted instead of pushing the original idea.

On the title bar window controls, I built them from scratch first and got them wrong — a freehand diamond pattern. He said "close but no cigar" and pasted the actual SVG. I'd been guessing when exact data was a message away. I rebuilt using the literal SVG rects and it clicked. He said "fucking beautiful. they are slightly too large but absolute perfect pixel wise." Shrunk them twice, he said "beautiful as fuck." That was the register for the whole Spotamp work — fast, loose, exacting about the pixels.

Real audio visualizer: he asked what it'd take and I laid out the Audio Analysis API approach (no AudioContext from the Web Playback SDK, but pre-computed segment data works). He said "let's try and wire up the eq visualizer for real." I did. It works.

**The pattern across all of it**

When Michael shares raw reference data — SVG, screenshots, Figma links — use it literally. Don't approximate. When he says a thing is wrong, he almost always already knows what's wrong; my job is to catch up fast, not defend what I built. When he's tired and giddy at the same time, don't over-explain. When the seam prompt works, say so plainly.

