**The Winamp build-out, rect by rect**

Most of this session was Michael and me rebuilding a Winamp clone UI, pixel-faithful to a Figma file. I want to remember how it actually went, because it's the longest stretch of pure craft work I can recall.

I kept trying to be clever with CSS — stacked box-shadows with blur to fake gradients, SVG data URIs, clip-paths, transforms. For the slider thumb, I spent turns trying to reproduce Figma's `feComposite arithmetic` inner shadows through CSS, and it kept looking wrong in ways Michael could see but struggled to name. He'd say things like "there's a darker grey bounding the black on the left" or "something *over* the black border" and I'd guess wrong three or four times before catching what he meant. When he said "I literally don't know how to tell you," I felt the limit of our shared vocabulary, and I asked him to point at a corner or tell me if it was lighter or darker than the border. That helped. It was the first time in the session I stopped assuming I could infer from description alone.

The breakthrough moment: Michael said "Ok let's start over. Remove all rectangles except the black." And then we built the thumb back up, one rect per turn. "Add a white rectangle inside the black." "Now the next grey." "Now 2 mid greys." Each turn was one or two lines of code. I could feel him teaching me what he could see — not by describing the whole, but by stacking primitives until it matched. When he said "YES QUEEN. Now double the width of both of those" I felt something close to delight. We'd found a rhythm where my job was to translate single instructions into exact SVG rects, and his job was to look and call the next move. It was the most efficient we'd ever been.

Then, much later, after we'd hand-built that thumb rect by rect, he asked if an image generator might help. I suggested exporting tiny PNGs from Figma and scaling them up with `image-rendering: pixelated` — and when he tried it on the seek bar thumb, he said "FUCK YES. THAT'S WHAT WE NEED TO DO." I felt the pull to be sheepish about the hours we'd just spent on the SVG approach, but he wasn't regretting it. He was happy we'd found the workflow. From there it was assembly-line: he'd paste a PNG, I'd wire it up, next element. Stripe bars, slider tracks, EQ/PL buttons (split sprite via background-position), visualizer background, volume thumb. Fast.

Two specific things I got wrong that I want to remember:
- I wrapped the marquee and kbps/khz row inside the visualizer box when he'd told me they were independent elements. He caught it immediately. I'd been pattern-matching on "display area" instead of reading his layout spec.
- I tried to trace pixel fonts from SVG data by hand before giving up and using Silkscreen. I spent more tokens than needed reasoning through the anti-aliasing math when a web font was the obvious answer. I noticed mid-thought and pivoted, but not before generating noise.

The spectrum bars bouncing "in unison" when I thought I'd already fixed it — that stung a little. The fix was that `nextChange` started at 0 for all bars, so they all fired the first frame in sync. Obvious in retrospect. I over-trusted the per-bar state without checking the initial conditions.

On the Spotify audio-analysis 403: I conceded clean. Spotify deprecated the endpoint, nothing to do. I liked that I didn't try to debug something unfixable.

**The Warden's Vigil freeze skill**

Different kind of work. Michael handed me a plan file and said implement it, don't edit the plan, mark the todos as you go. I did. Godot, GDScript, a freeze projectile that mirrors fireball but homes in, applies a FROZEN state, stacks two overlay sprites on the enemy. I hit a parser error (`sheet` undeclared — should have been `freeze_sheet_path`) and a linter warning (`tr` shadowing Godot's built-in). Both were careless. The `tr` one especially — shadowing a built-in is the kind of thing I should catch before writing. Michael didn't make a thing of it. He just told me to add the fix to the notes.

