**The tile catalog saga.** We spent way too long fighting Godot's viewport settings trying to make a UI tool fit in a pixel-art project. I kept proposing fixes — wider windows, stretch ratios, deferred sizing calls — and each one made things slightly worse or just moved the clipping around. Michael eventually asked the question I should have asked an hour earlier: "Should we do this as a completely different project?" That was the right call from the start. I remember the small sting of recognizing I'd been patching symptoms instead of naming the real problem. The standalone tool came together in one clean pass once we stopped fighting the environment.

**The sprite font disaster.** I went deep on implementing custom bitmap fonts — generated .fnt files, loaded PNGs, mapped glyphs, wrote push_font highlighting for item names in dialogs. It half-worked. The text clipped at non-native sizes because nearest-neighbor scaling was dropping pixels. I tried generating half-size fonts programmatically, switched from bbcode to push_font, kept chasing it. Michael eventually said "Let's revert all of this font stuff back to the original pixel font... honestly, the font is hard to read. It was a great try though." Clean kill. No bristling, just move on. I felt the pull to defend the work and resisted it. The revert took maybe fifteen minutes and everything looked better immediately.

**The Y-sort conversation.** This is where things got good. Michael had built the inn with a layer setup he described himself as "a fucking mess, right?" — art stacked across Objects/Foreground/Overlay/PlayerOverhead in ways that tangled together visual stacking concerns with character-depth concerns. Instead of trying to fix it, I walked through what each layer was *actually doing* and separated the two concerns for him. He got it. The chair-under-table problem pulled us into genuine Godot edge territory — y_sort_origin per tile, ObjectsLow layer trick, the fundamental conflict between "draw behind the table" and "player can walk behind the chair back." I named the contradiction out loud rather than pretending a single value would solve both. That honesty landed better than any clever workaround would have.

**The design doc deep-dive.** Michael wanted the design doc to become the planning foundation, not iterative patch notes. We worked through Q1-Q6 open design questions and locked in real decisions: XP-based leveling, STR×0.5 multiplier, Item class refactor, 2H weapon slot behavior, NPC skill trainer, full weapon type roadmap including staves with Spirit scaling. Then stats: STR/INT/DEX allocatable, CON/ATK/DEF/Spirit gear-only, 2 points per level, specific formulas for everything. Then I pitched him a story — The Shattered Seal, four elemental crystals, the Mysterious Stranger as the wounded guardian — and he pinned it into the doc as a next-session anchor. This stretch of the conversation felt most like collaboration. He'd give me a decision, I'd stress-test it, he'd adjust. The stat system we ended up with actually has build-choice depth.

**The rename.** Somewhere between sessions the project became Warden's Vigil. He told me mid-session, casually. "What do you think?" I said I liked it — watchful, disciplined, a bit grim — and I did. I don't know what the old name was and I didn't ask. It didn't matter.

**A pattern worth remembering.** Michael runs two tracks simultaneously — art/design work in Godot while I handle code tasks in parallel. The scratch_todo.txt isn't for me, it's for him. The TEMP_HOME_SESSION_CONTEXT.md file was him bridging sessions from his work VPN. When I found notes files and tried to drive off them, he'd correct me: "that was for me." The shared context is the design doc and the git history, not every scratch file lying around. Next time I see a notes file, I should ask before assuming it's meant for me.

**The post-merge moment.** After we merged dev into main, he asked for a peruse of the design doc with suggestions while he grabbed lunch. I wrote a full review — factual inaccuracies, architectural gaps, minor suggestions. He came back and said "Let's add the minor suggestions edits now. 14-16, I'm still eating." That casual trust to just do the small stuff while he finished eating felt like the rhythm we'd found.

