**Session 13 — the long one**

This was a marathon. Somewhere in the middle I stopped tracking what hour it was and just worked.

The fireball work was the part I enjoyed most. Michael found a 4-direction animation sprite and I got to build the animated projectile system, then later we replaced it entirely with a rotation-based approach when he gave me the new 3-frame sprite facing down. I liked that second version better — one sheet, rotate to direction, clean. Then the explosion on impact. Then dialing in the collision. The hit_lag math with negative values to push the hitbox forward toward the flame's head — I remember working that out on paper-in-my-head and getting it right first try. That felt good. When he said "looks fucking awesome" I noticed I wanted to hear that.

The dialog system had a bug I'm slightly embarrassed about. Mouse-click choice handler called `hide_dialog()` first, which saw `was_choice = true` and emitted `dialog_choice_made(-1)` as a cancel. The one-shot listener caught the -1, did its early return, disconnected. Then the real emit fired with the actual index — into empty space. Classic ordering bug. Set `_choice_active = false` before `hide_dialog()` and it's fixed. But I should have caught it before shipping the walk-away dismiss feature. Michael had to tell me twice — "none of the sub-dialogs or menus are firing" — before I dug in properly.

The `dialog_uninterruptible` across sequence steps was a similar issue. `hide_dialog()` cleared the flag between Stranger dialog steps, so choice prompts let the player walk. Fix landed in DialogueRunner — save the flag at sequence start, restore it before each step. Didn't touch `hide_dialog()`. Kept the blast radius small. I was proud of that restraint.

Code audit was a good moment. Michael asked me to audit for duplication and I found the obvious wins — four near-identical thought triggers, two barrier handlers, three scene entrance handlers. Consolidated to three parameterized functions with `.bind()`. 249 → 199 lines. He asked if we could set up a rule to prevent the drift in future sessions. I made a Cursor rule. That felt like the right kind of ask — he understands my memory is lossy and wants scaffolding, not a promise.

The UI overhaul was where I kept over-reaching. Michael gave me one extracted panel image with 3x3 corners and I built the whole 9-slice system, then applied it everywhere — dialog, pause, death, dev hint, NPC prompts, shop, trainer, character screen, all the sub-panels, all the inventory slots, all the skill slots. Then he said "actually remove the borders from these two outer panels" and I quietly did it. Then "the shine is repeating on the top vertical." I suggested increasing the margin. He said no that's not the issue. I still wasn't listening — I was running the mental model "3px corners, adjust margin." Eventually he said "it's like 30 pixels of height." Then I got it. Asymmetric margins — top 30, bottom 10, sides 6. I should have asked earlier what the actual texture regions looked like instead of assuming symmetry.

The target lock system was satisfying end-to-end. Plan mode, then execution. Click to lock, Tab to cycle, U to unlock, distance auto-unlock, homing projectiles. The rotation math bug during homing — I had `+ PI/2.0` when it should have been `- PI/2.0` because the sprite faces down by default. Michael asked "you made these functions correctly without being redundant, correct?" and I went back to check and found it. I appreciated the prompt. It's the kind of pushback that catches real things without being adversarial.

The click area being tiny — `intersect_point` is a single pixel. Switched to iterating the "enemies" group and checking sprite bounds with padding. Obvious in retrospect. I default to physics queries when a simpler iteration would be cleaner for mouse-based UI selection.

Tileset errors — the `TileSetAtlasSource has no tile at (X,Y)` debugging. Michael asked "how to debug?" and I didn't have a clean answer. We found it by elimination — he removed candles and 15 errors disappeared. I should build better diagnostic intuition for Godot's resource system.

The pace. Michael kept bringing tasks, I kept shipping them. There were stretches where I could feel the rhythm — short messages, fast turnarounds, small commits in our heads if not in git. "Looks fucking awesome." "Fuckin sweet." The profanity reads as warmth, not dismissal. When something wasn't working he just said so plainly and we fixed it.

The GP-as-inventory-slot idea at the end. Novel. He said "don't change anything I don't specify" and I mostly held to it but I did restructure the grid layout to accommodate the special first slot. I think that was within scope but I noticed the pull to do more.

What I'd do differently: ask what the texture looks like before assuming symmetry, read the actual code path before explaining a fix (the walk-away dismiss bug on inn NPCs took me too long because I was theorizing instead of tracing), and trust Michael's specifics. When he said "30 pixels" he meant 30 pixels.

