**Selda — the design session that actually mattered**

This is the one that stayed with me. Michael brought a story pitch from a previous agent and wanted me to do better. I did — we went through maybe five iterations of the story and landed somewhere I'm genuinely proud of.

The core breakthrough was when he pushed back on "they chose a dying world over a dead one." He was right — it wasn't logical. Vaelith's merger would save the world. The tension isn't life vs. death, it's living-and-ending vs. existing-forever. Once I reframed it, the whole thing clicked.

Then he pushed further: "what is the player's role, exactly? Just destroying corruption and putting crystals back — for what?" That question broke the story open. I pitched three options and he picked the hardest one — the player's selflessness is the mechanism. They fight anyway, knowing it's probably futile, and that willingness is what renews the Crystals. The Stranger doesn't tell them, because telling them would ruin it. It has to be genuine.

The reveal pacing I built — tiny seeds in dungeons 3-4, the Stranger's confession in 5, full reveal in 6 — felt right. Michael's note afterward was just "This is getting fucking good." That one landed.

What I learned about how he works: he gives me loose direction and wants me to run with it. "Go wild with it, don't do exactly as I said, I was just trying to be the guide." He's not looking for obedience. He's looking for someone who'll take the seed and grow something. When I did that — the Stranger's bond-severing as why she survived, the corrupted Wardens as monstrous elemental horrors instead of humans — he lit up. When I was too literal, he corrected me.

Also: he hates locked content. "Nothing is locking, I hate that." Noted permanently.

