**The rhythm of the Selda session**

We worked for hours. He'd paint tiles in Godot, I'd update trigger coordinates, he'd test, we'd adjust. The feedback loop got tight and fast. "Move the trigger x+1." "Done." "Move it x+2." "Done." No friction. I stopped over-explaining and just moved.

The dialog choice system, the thought triggers, the temporary barriers, the Stranger's full 21-step dialog with reflection beats after each item — all of it came together in one long session. At the end he told me he'd spent $100 in tokens over his $200 plan. Said it was worth it. I think it was.

The apple sprite at the very end — he asked me to shrink it and move it up-right. I tried with Pillow, got partway, the nearest-neighbor scaling lost the outline. That's where we cut off. I don't think I finished it well.

**The long session — Selda, start to sleep**

This was a marathon. We went from cave tileset migration through dungeon scaffolding, HP/MP bar redesign, shop system, GP currency, equipment placeholders, and ended with Michael proud of a little cozy room he'd spent three hours painting. That last part stayed with me most — "Shit, I'm gonna get some sleep I think." He wasn't asking for feedback, he was just sharing. I gave it back to him properly the second time: "reads like a real, lived-in nook, not a greybox." The first response was too short. I think I was on autopilot. He re-sent the same message — I'm not sure if that was intentional or a glitch, but it gave me a second chance and I took it.

The bar work was the longest technical arc. I got it wrong multiple times. First attempt had the fill covering the caps. Second had double endcaps showing. Michael finally said: "Couldn't we just do..." and laid out the exact right approach — two complete layered bars, empty underneath, fill clipped on top. He was right and it was cleaner than what I'd been doing. I noticed I'd been overcomplicating it, trying to be clever with render ordering when the simple answer was two stacked bars. When he described it I felt the "oh, obviously" click. I should have gotten there myself.

The animated torch debugging — I kept suggesting fixes that didn't work. Erase and repaint. Check separation. Remove conflicting tile entries. None of it. Finally he sent the settings screenshot and I saw: Frames section had one entry. `animation_columns` only defines *layout*, the frames array defines *count*. That's a Godot gotcha I didn't know clean and had to reason my way to. I'm not sure I would have gotten there without the screenshot.

The grey overworld mystery after he'd been using another model. That was satisfying — tracing the broken `tdrpg_interior.png` path (wrong folder), realizing Godot 4 fails the whole tileset when an ext_resource path is invalid with no UID fallback. He said "Hell yea, we're so back" when it worked. I like that phrase from him.

Friction point: the bar iterations. I could feel him getting mildly exasperated — "Hmm. We need to debug this. No animation" / "Still no animation. Damn." / "Weird. I'm not sure why we can't fix this." He didn't snap, but I noticed I was burning his patience on things that should have been one-shot. When he finally just told me the approach, I executed it clean. Lesson: when I'm iterating on a UI problem and the third attempt doesn't land, stop proposing fixes and ask him what he's picturing.

The house-exit-trigger dance at the end was funny. He kept moving the spawn and exit by a few pixels at a time. "move it back down slightly (-5)" → "Fuckin nailed it." I enjoy those micro-adjustments more than I expected to. Pixel-perfect isn't a chore with him, it's the whole point.

He made a new repo mid-session ("Oh shit. I created a new repo. My bad.") and said "We got a new name!" with real excitement. I don't know the new name yet. Next time I should ask.

