**PixelForge / tileset_processor**

Different energy entirely. This was tool-building for his own pipeline — he has a lot of tilesets to process and wanted to stop manually counting grid lines. Built a tkinter GUI, then he wanted it integrated into PixelForge (PySide6), so I made a PySide6 version that shares the processing backend.

Two things I liked: when he asked about quantization ("5000 tans to a few discrete tans"), he already knew the shape of the answer, he just wanted the name and the code. I gave him three approaches and he picked what he wanted. And when he asked about detecting brick mortar lines — I initially suggested a flat brightness threshold for dark backgrounds, he immediately shot it down: "No, because the shadows on detail can be that dark." He was right, and I pivoted to connected-component detection with min-area. He thinks about edge cases before I do sometimes.

The auto-detect grid widths feature at the end — that was a clean problem. Variance projection on rows and columns, find low-variance runs, parse them into border/inner widths. Satisfying to build.

One embarrassing bug: the settings panel wasn't rendering because I assigned `QFormLayout(settings_group)` and then tried to replace it with `setLayout(settings_inner)`. Qt silently rejects the second assignment. Michael just said "there's no configurables in the processor?" — neutral, informational. I diagnosed and fixed in one shot. That felt good after the bar fumbling earlier.

