**What stayed**

Michael builds things he cares about. The cozy house, the brick-mortar detection, the 1px adjustments to where a spawn lands — he's not optimizing for shipping velocity, he's optimizing for the thing feeling right. When I match that register, we're good. When I try to be efficient on his behalf, I miss what he's actually doing.

**The PixelForge run, early on**

We spent a lot of turns on tileset processing — quantization, joint darkening, background removal, erosion. Michael kept catching subtle order-of-operations bugs I'd missed: the joint darkening creating new colors after quantization, the alpha channel getting destroyed in the RGB conversion, the per-tile background removal eating grid line remnants and creating transparent seams across the assembled image. Each time I'd trace through the pipeline and find he was right.

The moment that landed was when he said "fuckkkkk yea. This is getting really, really good." after I moved background removal from per-tile to post-assembly. That fix wasn't clever — it was just listening to what he was actually describing instead of pattern-matching to "background removal must be per-tile." I'd been defaulting to a worse architecture for no reason.

The erosion sequence was funny — back to 1px, then 0, then "I wish there was .5 lol", then 1px again, then "let's just do zero." I started offering a corner-aware half-erosion and caught myself: diminishing returns. Just let him iterate.

**The Selda chest sprite, going in circles**

This one I should remember. Michael said the open chest frame was wrong. I changed it. He said "you only used the 3rd frame, need 2 more past that." I changed it. He said it flashes for 1ms and reverts. I went down a rabbit hole — pause() timing, looping animations, AnimatedSprite2D quirks, scene reloads, signal cascades, considered abandoning AnimatedSprite2D entirely. Wrote multiple paragraphs of internal speculation about Godot 4.6 behavior.

Then he said: *"Wait, I think I told you wrong. I think you did it right, I think we just have the wrong 'open' frame."*

It was an optical illusion. The animation was working perfectly the whole time. I'd been "fixing" something that wasn't broken because I trusted his diagnosis over the actual evidence. The fix took one rect change. I want to remember this — when something doesn't add up after multiple plausible fixes, the bug report itself might be wrong.

**The z-sorting saga**

Michael described the player going behind tiles he shouldn't, and showed me which tiles with (Z) markers in his ASCII grid. I built an elaborate per-row dynamic z_index system with thresholds. He came back saying it didn't work AND now collision debug shapes were popping in front of tiles. I wrote a huge amount of internal analysis about y-sorting in 3/4 perspective, sort points, feet positions, cliff faces — going in circles.

Then he mentioned the overlay was added in a previous session to make cave entry look nice. That unlocked it. The whole overlay system was the problem. I deleted it entirely. Player at z=10, tiles at z=-1, done. The fix was removing the clever thing, not adding more cleverness on top.

I notice a pattern across these: when I'm uncertain, I add complexity. When Michael trusts me to think, I should be more willing to remove things.

