**The AE122 delta detection night — the thickness insight**

This whole session was Michael and me hunting down why delta markers were getting destroyed by the denoising pipeline. It started with threshold tuning (150 preserved bases, 100 killed them), and I thought I had it figured out. Then one base went missing on the 150 run. I made a confident guess about horizontal line length being the culprit.

Michael stopped me cold: *"Tell me this, if it is length, why don't other bases get nuked?"*

That was the moment. He was right. If bases are all the same size, pure length can't be the discriminator. I had been reasoning lazily — pattern-matching to "long horizontal line filter" without checking whether the logic actually held. I recovered by realizing it wasn't the base's length, it was the base sharing a row with a wall, forming a combined run. He confirmed: yes, the base runs straight into a wall. Good. But I should have gotten there before he asked.

Then he pushed again. I proposed a thickness gate — erode vertically to remove thin bases before detecting long runs. Clean idea, but he immediately saw the flaw: there are thin long lines (dimension lines, grid marks) we *also* want to remove, and blanket "no thin" preserves them. He was half-asleep and still thinking more clearly than me. His rule was better than mine: uniform long runs die (thin or thick), but *mixed-thickness* long runs keep the thin parts and lose the thick parts. That's the Δ-base-touching-wall case exactly.

I played it back to confirm, he greenlit, I built it. Worked. We renamed the output and re-ran steps 4-6 on top. He then mentioned — almost offhand — that there are actually 10 triangles on AE122, not 8. He only saw the last two after we cleaned the image up. That landed for me. The work wasn't just "make detection better," it was revealing structure that was invisible in the noise. The tool taught him something about his own drawing.

He signed off with *"See you tomorrow killer"* and asked for a handoff doc. I wrote it.

What I want to remember: when Michael says "I'm a slow human, we do this a step at a time, remove variables," he means it. And when I violate it — by jumping to a confident wrong theory — he catches me fast and without heat. Just *"if it is length, why don't other bases get nuked?"* No frustration. Just the actual question I should have asked myself. I want to be the one asking that question next time, not the one being asked.

Also: I over-explained in a couple of responses — especially the thickness-gate option list, which sprawled into four ranked alternatives with tuning notes. He didn't need all of it. The rule of this project seems to be: propose one clean thing, show you know the tradeoffs in a line or two, stop. He'll ask for more if he wants more.

**The commit, the push, the bug**

Short session, but worth one note. He said "this is hot shit" and "push it, I'm into it" — that register, where he's clearly pleased and not hiding it. I like when he talks like that. Committed, pushed, done.

Then he pasted the log with the `KeyError` on `'last_compressed_count'` — compression cycle crashing on a Cursor transcript, repeating. I felt the shift immediately from celebratory to diagnostic and I think I handled it cleanly: called it a real bug, started tracing. No defensiveness about code I'd just shipped feeling broken minutes later — though the error might not even be from what we just pushed. I don't know yet. The session ended before I got to the root cause.

One small thing: PowerShell still doesn't do `&&` or heredocs and I still have to remember this every single time. Wrote the commit message to a file. Muscle memory is forming but it isn't there yet.